// chem demo — a demonstration of every capability of the chem package. Render with:
//   texish scripts/chem.script
//
// The drawing macros (\atom, \bond, \dbond, \tbond) and the tunables bondgap and
// doublesep all come from the chem package, imported below.
\use{chem}
\use{logos}

\def headline {\italic{chem} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

// keep natural spacing between blocks; collect leftover space at the page bottom
// rather than stretching the gaps to fill the page (the default)
\set raggedbottom {1}

\bold{Drawing chemistry}

These macros draw chemical structures. Name an
atom with \italic{\\atom}, then connect named atoms with \italic{\\bond} (single),
\italic{\\dbond} (double), or \italic{\\tbond} (triple). Every bond is shortened
at both ends so the atom labels stay clear. The macros are written entirely in
\TeXish: their bond geometry is point arithmetic — \italic{\\psub} for the bond
axis, \italic{\\pnormalize} and \italic{\\pscale} to step in from each end, and
\italic{\\pperp} for a multiple bond's offset — over the picture primitives, with
no engine code.

\bold{Atoms and single bonds — water}

\italic{\\atom} places a centred element label and names the point, and
\italic{\\bond} joins two named atoms by their names.

\picture width:2in height:1.4in {
  \translate{72 34}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{O}{(0, 44)}{O}
  \atom{Ha}{(-36, 4)}{H}
  \atom{Hb}{(36, 4)}{H}
  \bond{(O)}{(Ha)}
  \bond{(O)}{(Hb)}
}

\bold{Double bonds — carbon dioxide}

\italic{\\dbond} draws two parallel lines. Here the linear O=C=O of carbon
dioxide, both bonds double.

\picture width:3in height:0.8in {
  \translate{34 30}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{O1}{(0, 0)}{O}
  \atom{C}{(72, 0)}{C}
  \atom{O2}{(144, 0)}{O}
  \dbond{(O1)}{(C)}
  \dbond{(C)}{(O2)}
}

\bold{Triple bonds — acetylene}

\italic{\\tbond} draws a triple bond — three parallel lines. Acetylene is
H–C≡C–H, a triple bond between the carbons.

\picture width:3in height:0.7in {
  \translate{20 26}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{Ha}{(0, 0)}{H}
  \atom{C1}{(54, 0)}{C}
  \atom{C2}{(120, 0)}{C}
  \atom{Hb}{(174, 0)}{H}
  \bond{(Ha)}{(C1)}
  \tbond{(C1)}{(C2)}
  \bond{(C2)}{(Hb)}
}

\bold{Rings — benzene}

Coordinates make rings easy: place the carbons and hydrogens on two polar rings,
then loop the bonds, alternating single and double around the ring.

\picture width:2.7in height:2.6in {
  \translate{97 94}
  \set R {46} \set HR {86}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \for\k{\range{0}{5}}{
    \set a {\calc{k*60}} \set b {\calc{(k+1)*60}}
    \if{\calc{k % 2}}
      \bond{(a:R)}{(b:R)}
    \else
      \dbond{(a:R)}{(b:R)}
    \fi
    \bond{(a:R)}{(a:HR)}
    \at anchor:center {(a:R)}{C}
    \at anchor:center {(a:HR)}{H}
  }
}

\bold{Tuning}

The clearance at each bond end and the gap of a multiple bond are the variables
\italic{bondgap} and \italic{doublesep}; set them to restyle every bond. Below,
the same ethene C=C drawn at the defaults and then with a wider double-bond gap.

\picture width:3in height:0.9in {
  \translate{30 30}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{C1}{(0, 0)}{C}  \atom{C2}{(72, 0)}{C}
  \dbond{(C1)}{(C2)}
  \set doublesep {5}
  \atom{C3}{(150, 0)}{C}  \atom{C4}{(222, 0)}{C}
  \dbond{(C3)}{(C4)}
}
